      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "$\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}({cot}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "We multiply out the parentheses and then use the identity ${cot}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}({cot}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {cot}^{2}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({csc}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
