      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}3\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x{cos}^{2}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We use the identity $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}3\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x{cos}^{2}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=2x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-sin\\,(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
